const COURSE_11_FINAL_EXTRA_DEF=[{ q:'Your neighbor’s Wi-Fi on the same channel slows your network even though you aren’t connected to it. Why?', opts:['the air is a shared medium: only one device may transmit at a time, others on the channel wait','the neighbor’s AP secretly joins yours and silently redirects part of your bandwidth to itself','your AP has to decrypt and re-encrypt every one of the neighbor’s frames, which slows it down'], correct:0, why:'A radio channel is a shared medium – whoever wants to transmit must wait for it to be free. Foreign traffic on the same channel therefore steals airtime from your devices too, without joining your network. The fix is another channel or a less crowded band (5/6 GHz).' },{ q:'A laptop user walks from one AP’s range into another’s and the connection survives. What made that possible, and who decided to switch?', opts:['roaming: both APs offer the same SSID and security, and the client itself decides to switch','the APs handed the client over by wire after agreeing between themselves; it never noticed','the router watched the client’s signal strength and ordered it to change APs at the right time'], correct:0, why:'Roaming works when several APs broadcast the same network (SSID + security) and share the same wired backbone. But the client decides when to switch based on signal strength – which is why different devices roam with different eagerness.' },{ q:'You want to extend coverage upstairs. How does a mesh with its own backhaul differ from a plain repeater?', opts:['a mesh carries data between nodes over a dedicated path; a repeater re-sends all on one channel, halving throughput','a repeater is always faster because it only copies packets and needn’t re-encrypt them like a mesh','a mesh only works with a cable between every node, while a repeater even needs no power at all'], correct:0, why:'A repeater receives and re-transmits on the same channel, so every transfer runs twice and throughput roughly halves. Mesh nodes have a dedicated backhaul between them (radio or wired), so they don’t halve the clients’ speed.' }];
